TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Introduction","issue":"The report names likely customer agencies but does not explicitly identify a mission operator; the Australian ground facility is not itself an operator."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The proposed Vega E launch and rideshare arrangement are speculative rather than a confirmed mission opportunity. Compatibility of the spacecraft and deployment into the specified orbit is not demonstrated."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The reported RAAN does not by itself indicate the launch facility's location; RAAN is an inertial orbital orientation. An epoch and a more complete state specification are needed to interpret the orbital elements."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The ascending-node local solar time is not specified. Sun-synchronism alone does not establish that the satellite will image the reef in daylight or on the descending leg."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The claim that the near-polar orbit passes over almost the entire Earth is overstated: at 97.79° inclination, the ground track reaches only about 82.21° latitude."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The sensor's swath, ground sample distance, spectral capability, and ability to detect bleaching through water are not established. The proposed 600 km altitude and fluid-lensing application therefore lack a demonstrated imaging-feasibility basis."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table includes a duplicated access period and inconsistent access numbering, which weakens confidence in the reported mean and total contact statistics."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The assertion that access frequency is maximized by selecting a facility close to the poles is not generally valid for this mission; access depends on orbit geometry, site latitude, elevation mask, and operational constraints."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The report first describes an EOL manoeuvre, then states the spacecraft has no thrusters and proposes a drag sail. Drag-sail sizing, deorbit time, and compliance with disposal guidance are not evaluated."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"The report cites Wikipedia as a source despite the task's explicit exclusion, and its references are largely bare URLs without complete bibliographic information. Table citations such as [50]–[74] do not correspond to the listed bibliography."}],"overall":"The report presents a coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept and uses STK outputs to support parts of the orbit and communications design. Several required topics are covered, but important design validation is missing, the EOL plan is incomplete, and the report has significant citation and figure/table presentation shortcomings.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Explains the reef-monitoring mission and its Australian benefit, gives a rationale for using one spacecraft, and identifies likely users and funding. The operator is not explicitly named, and the rationale and user discussion are not supported with literature consistently, so this falls short of the highest standard.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The satellite, nicknamed ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), is operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Addresses launch, orbit, manoeuvres, ground control and access periods, duration, and EOL, with STK-derived results and orbit screenshots. However, orbital elements and mission operations are incomplete, the launch choice is speculative, sensor feasibility is not established, and EOL is underdeveloped. No software project files are evident in the submission.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarizes the principal design choices and connects them to the mission purpose. It is brief and largely restates the report; it does not draw detailed conclusions from the access analysis or resolve the design uncertainties.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable structure, executive summary, contents, headings, figures, and tables. Presentation is weakened by grammar and typographical errors, tables and figures without consistent numbering and titles, incomplete bibliography details, inconsistent citation numbering, and use of Wikipedia as a source.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Launch","quote":"To compare existing spaceflight mission plans, a wiki sorted directory – ‘Timeline of spaceflight’ – will be referred to throughout the report [6]."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"The first fifteen access periods are listed above."}]}]}
